# Commit Message Convention

## Purpose

Commit messages in this repository must communicate intent clearly and professionally.
They exist to explain *why a change exists*, not to narrate activity.

Poor commit history signals low engineering discipline and is unacceptable.

---

## Commit Message Format

All commits MUST follow the Conventional Commits standard.

<type>(scope): <imperative summary>

<optional body explaining why, not how>

### Rules
- Use the imperative mood (“add”, “fix”, “remove”, not “added”, “fixed”).
- Keep the summary under 72 characters.
- Do not explain implementation details.
- Do not combine unrelated changes in one commit.

---

## Allowed Commit Types

| Type | Meaning |
|-----|--------|
| feat | New user-facing functionality |
| fix | Bug fix |
| refactor | Code change without behavior change |
| perf | Performance improvement |
| test | Add or modify tests |
| docs | Documentation only |
| chore | Tooling, config, non-runtime changes |
| ci | CI/CD pipeline changes |
| build | Build system or dependency changes |

No other types are permitted.

---

## Examples

### Feature

feat(auth): add JWT-based authentication middleware

### Bug Fix

fix(api): handle null userId in order creation

### Refactor

refactor(lambda): extract shared validation logic

### Performance

perf(dynamodb): add GSI for customerId queries

### Documentation

docs(readme): document local setup and environment variables

---

## What Is Forbidden

The following commit messages are unacceptable:

updated stuff
fix
final changes
working now
minor edits

These indicate lack of intent and will be rejected in serious codebases.

---

## The Golden Rule

- The summary explains **what changed**.
- The body explains **why it changed**.
- Never explain *how*. The code already does that.

---

## Using AI Assistance

AI may be used only to refine wording.
AI must not invent intent, infer changes, or summarize large unfocused commits.

If you cannot explain the change in two sentences, do not commit yet.

---

## Breaking Changes

Breaking changes must be explicitly marked.

feat(api)!: remove legacy v1 endpoints

BREAKING CHANGE: Clients must migrate to v2 endpoints.

---

## Final Note

A clean commit history:
- Speeds up code reviews
- Protects the project during audits
- Signals senior-level engineering discipline
